# SHARQ — Complete Project Handoff

**SHARQ = SHift-Add Routed Quantization**

This document is a full transfer of everything accumulated on the SHARQ project. It is written to be read cold, with no prior context. If you are a new assistant picking this up: read Sections 1–4 carefully. Those contain the actual idea. Everything after that is supporting detail, plans, and history.

---

## Part 0 — The one-paragraph version

SHARQ is a way to quantize the weights of a large language model so that the hardware never has to multiply. Every weight level is forced to be a sum of at most two powers of two. That restriction means a weight times an activation can be computed with two small exponent additions and one floating-point add, instead of a full multiplier. The set of legal levels is small enough (11 values) that the software can search *every* possible codebook exhaustively and pick the best one for each layer. Which levels a given model uses is stored as a few dozen bits in a register — not baked into the gates — so one fixed silicon design serves every model, every layer, and every bit-width (4, 3, or 2 bits). The paper contribution is the processing element (PE) itself, aimed at a computer architecture venue.

---

## Part 1 — The core idea

### 1.1 The problem being solved

In an LLM inference accelerator with 4-bit weights and FP16 activations (W4A16), people usually assume the 4-bit weight is the cheap part. It is. The expensive part is the FP16 multiply-accumulate hardware sitting behind it. If you want a smaller, cooler chip, the multiplier is the thing to attack.

There is an existing trick for this. If a weight value is a power of two, then multiplying an FP16 activation by it is just adding a constant to the activation's exponent field. No multiplier needed. The mantissa bits pass straight through on wires. **This is the single most important mechanism in the project, and it is not ours** — it is MixPE's Equation 7 (arXiv:2411.16158). SHARQ builds on it and must cite it as prior work.

MixPE applies this to standard uniform INT4 weights. A uniform 4-bit weight can be any value from 0 to 15. Written in binary, some of those values have up to four 1-bits (15 = 8+4+2+1). So MixPE has to build hardware that can handle the worst case: **four** exponent-scale units feeding a **three-adder tree, two levels deep**. It cannot shrink that, because it has to support every uniform level.

### 1.2 The SHARQ move

Do not accept the uniform level set. Choose the level set to match what cheap hardware can do.

**Every SHARQ weight magnitude is `a + b` where `a, b ∈ {0, 1, 2, 4, 8}` and the two terms are distinct.**

Equivalently, and this is the cleanest way to state it in a paper: **SHARQ's codebook is the popcount-≤2 subset of UINT4.** Take the 16 uniform 4-bit values and keep only the ones with at most two 1-bits set.

That gives exactly **11 legal magnitudes**:

```
{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12}
```

Broken down:

| Level | Term A | Term B | en2 | selA | selB | Tier |
|------:|-------:|-------:|----:|-----:|-----:|------|
| 0 | — | — | 0 | — | — | zero |
| 1 | 1 | — | 0 | 00 | — | Tier-1 |
| 2 | 2 | — | 0 | 01 | — | Tier-1 |
| 3 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 00 | 01 | Tier-2 |
| 4 | 4 | — | 0 | 10 | — | Tier-1 |
| 5 | 1 | 4 | 1 | 00 | 10 | Tier-2 |
| 6 | 2 | 4 | 1 | 01 | 10 | Tier-2 |
| 8 | 8 | — | 0 | 11 | — | Tier-1 |
| 9 | 1 | 8 | 1 | 00 | 11 | Tier-2 |
| 10 | 2 | 8 | 1 | 01 | 11 | Tier-2 |
| 12 | 4 | 8 | 1 | 10 | 11 | Tier-2 |

**The illegal values in 0–15 are 7, 11, 13, 14, and 15.** Each would need three or more terms. The software search never picks them, so the hardware never has to represent them.

- **Tier-1** levels use one term. The second datapath is clock-gated off and the adder is bypassed.
- **Tier-2** levels use both terms. This is where the one FP16 add happens.

Because every magnitude is an exact small integer in `[0, 12]` and the sign is a separate bit, the full signed range is `[−12, +12]`, all exact integers.

### 1.3 Why this wins

MixPE must provision 4 exponent-scale units and a depth-2, 3-adder tree.
SHARQ provably needs **2 exponent-scale units and a depth-1, 1-adder tree.**

At A16 that adder is a *full FP16 adder*: exponent compare, mantissa alignment shift, 12-bit add, leading-zero detect, round, renormalize. It is the dominant cell. The exponent adder next to it is a 5-bit integer add — roughly 30–50 gates. So cutting three FP16 adders down to one, and gating that one off for every Tier-1 weight, is where the area and dynamic-power win lives.

Two structural facts to lean on, both exact by construction and not dependent on any synthesis run:

1. **Adder count: 1 vs 3.**
2. **Tree depth: 1 vs 2.** Half the combinational depth means frequency headroom.

A third, softer point: dynamic power scales with the fraction of Tier-2 weights in the model, because Tier-1 weights bypass the adder entirely. That fraction is a measurable property of a quantized model and is worth reporting.

### 1.4 The one-sentence pitch

> MixPE removed the multiplier. SHARQ also removes most of the adder tree — by making the codebook, not the datapath, absorb the complexity.

### 1.5 The second half of the idea: routing, not lookup

The 11 legal magnitudes are more than any single model needs at once, especially at 3 or 2 bits. So the map from code to level is **programmable**, while the datapath that realizes any legal level is **fixed**.

The critical distinction, and the reason the "R" in SHARQ is *Routed*:

> **The table stores routing decisions — which shift terms fire — not coefficient values.**

It is not a value LUT. The hardware never stores an FP16 number and never looks one up. It stores a handful of mux-select and enable bits. This matters enormously for the paper, because a value LUT would make every 4-bit non-uniform codebook cost exactly the same, which would erase SHARQ's entire hardware argument. See Section 7.3.

