## Part 6 — Positioning, related work, and venue

### 6.1 Venue

**Target: HPCA, MICRO, ISCA, or ASPLOS.** Secondary: DAC, ICCAD, DATE.

The reasoning matters. This started as an MLSys or ICASSP idea. It moved to architecture venues once it became clear that **the real silicon PE is the primary contribution, not a GPU kernel.** MLSys would demand a kernel or prototype; AAAI would treat it as an incremental algorithms paper next to APoT and call it a day. An architecture venue is where "we co-designed a codebook and a PE, here is the synthesized area" is the expected currency.

### 6.2 The competitive landscape

| Work | What it is | Relationship to SHARQ |
|------|-----------|----------------------|
| **MixPE** (arXiv:2411.16158) | Mixed-precision PE using FP16 exponent scaling (their Eq. 7) | **The single most important reference.** Closest competitor, structural template for the paper, and the hardware baseline to beat. SHARQ uses their primitive; the delta is the popcount-≤2 codebook constraint and the programmable enable table. |
| **ShiftAddLLM** | Shift-add LLM inference, BCQ with learned scaling factors | Non-uniform, more representational freedom than SHARQ. **Critical fact: its energy numbers come from a borrowed Eyeriss energy model, not real hardware.** It is a GPU paper. This is a meaningful competitive distinction in SHARQ's favor. |
| **PoTPTQ** | Additive power-of-two levels | Non-uniform, closest algorithmic cousin. Must be re-run under SHARQ's protocol. |
| **APoT** | Additive powers of two | The prior art that most threatens the novelty claim. Needs a clear, explicit answer in the related-work section. |
| **NF4 / SqueezeLLM** | Unconstrained non-uniform codebooks | Accuracy upper bound with no hardware story. |
| **LUT-GEMM / LUT Tensor Core** | Lookup-based decode | The alternative implementation a reviewer will raise. See 7.3. |
| **GPTQ / AWQ / BoA** | Standard uniform W4A16 PTQ | The accuracy baseline. BoA is also the framework SHARQ is built on. |

### 6.3 The correct framing — Pareto, not accuracy superiority

**Say this, and do not overclaim:**

> SHARQ sits at the most hardware-constrained end of the non-uniform spectrum by design. The claim is *comparable accuracy at strictly lower real silicon cost* than ShiftAddLLM and PoTPTQ — not accuracy superiority over them.

SHARQ deliberately has fewer degrees of freedom than BCQ or additive-PoT methods. Claiming to beat them on accuracy would be fighting on the axis where SHARQ is designed to lose. The win is the Pareto frontier: MixPE's Figure 7 axis is SNR versus normalized area-power, and SHARQ should push that frontier past MixPE-A16.

Similarly: **at iso-throughput and iso-clock, a SHARQ PE and a MixPE PE both retire one MAC per cycle.** There is no raw single-PE latency claim to make. If you want a headline "X× speedup," you must explicitly *reinvest* the area savings into more PEs per tile and say so. The depth-1 adder tree may also allow a higher clock, but that needs B1 to confirm.

### 6.4 The accuracy burden

MixPE-A16 keeps all 16 uniform levels, so **its accuracy is the W4 baseline for free.** SHARQ drops five levels and therefore owes the iso-accuracy argument. It has to be explicit and head-to-head: same W4A16, group 128, PPL within ε of uniform, at lower area-power.

One genuine upside of A16: weight-only quantization is exactly the regime the whole PTQ literature reports (GPTQ, AWQ, BoA, ShiftAddLLM are all W4A16). So the **accuracy** table is apples-to-apples with the algorithms literature while the **hardware** table is versus MixPE-A16. Two coherent comparisons, not a mismatch.

### 6.5 Baseline reuse strategy

- **BoA results: copy directly.** SHARQ uses BoA's pipeline, so the protocol is identical and comparability is exact. No need to re-run.
- **ShiftAddLLM and PoTPTQ: must be re-run independently under SHARQ's protocol.** These are the closest non-uniform competitors, and reviewers will scrutinize those rows hardest.

